# Allot provider

Allocate IPv4, IPv6 and integer capacity through the shared Allot library. The
provider calls the library directly; it does not run the CLI or modify Terraform
backend state.

## Configure storage

```hcl
provider "allot" {
  store = "/absolute/path/to/allocations"
}
```

`store` accepts a local path, `file:///path`, or `s3://bucket/prefix`, and defaults
to `ALLOT_STORE` when omitted. S3 buckets must already exist. The local backend
creates its directory when needed.

| Argument | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| `store` | Local directory or S3 URL; defaults to `ALLOT_STORE` |
| `endpoint` | Optional S3-compatible endpoint |
| `region` | S3 signing region; otherwise SDK configuration |
| `profile` | Optional AWS shared configuration profile |
| `path_style` | Use S3 path-style addressing; defaults to false |

For R2 or another compatible endpoint:

```hcl
provider "allot" {
  store      = "s3://my-bucket/allot"
  endpoint   = "https://ACCOUNT.r2.cloudflarestorage.com"
  region     = "auto"
  path_style = true
}
```

Credentials use AWS SDK for Go v2's standard environment, profile and role sources.
Do not include keys in store URLs. Terraform backend authentication is independent;
both can use the same execution environment, but backend-only profile, role and
endpoint settings are not automatically passed to this provider.

## Resources and references

- [allot_space](resources/space.md) owns one root allocation scope.
- [allot_claim](resources/claim.md) owns child capacity inside a root or another claim.

Every allocation has one UUID in `id`. Pass `.ref` to a child's `within`: it carries
the root key, root UUID and parent UUID. No space listing or global index is needed
to resolve parent references. These references also establish Terraform graph
dependencies so children are destroyed before their parents.

Actual bounds are exposed through `.result.start` and `.result.end`. Singleton
allocations also expose `.result.value`; CIDR-aligned IP intervals expose
`.result.cidr`. Other result fields are null. Integer results are decimal strings;
use `tonumber` when a consumer requires a Terraform number.

Changing a key, parent, domain or allocation bounds requires replacement. A
compatible automatic request can describe an existing claim without reallocating
it; see [claim selector rules](resources/claim.md). The core does not support
in-place moves or resizes. Destroy calls release, which fails if children remain.
There is no recursive release, retirement state or cooldown.

## Identity and recovery

Planning and reading do not allocate. Create generates a fresh UUID and uses the
core's idempotent CAS operations. An uncertain write reports its UUID and preserves
partial resource state where possible. A process exit before Terraform saves state
can still leave a committed allocation: inspect it with the CLI and explicitly
import the selected UUID. A name match never silently adopts an allocation.

Import identifiers are `SPACE_KEY/ALLOCATION_UUID`, split at the final slash so
space keys can contain slashes. Import restores the original request and its
references. Released UUIDs cannot be imported as live resources.

Ledger access is controlled by backend credentials or local filesystem access.
UUIDs fence stale operations; they are not authentication credentials. Use the same
store and prefix consistently, and withdraw consumers before releasing capacity.

